WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.357
Day 26 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.907 --> 00:00:08.869
Performance (2): load testing an LLM endpoint with k6.

00:00:09.419 --> 00:00:14.285
A staged load test against an LLM endpoint, with token and goodput metrics, to find

00:00:14.285 --> 00:00:16.689
the point where the system stops scaling.

00:00:17.989 --> 00:00:22.473
With yesterday's baseline and targets you can now ask the question that matters to

00:00:22.473 --> 00:00:26.683
whoever operates the system: how many users it supports before the experience

00:00:26.683 --> 00:00:27.175
degrades.

00:00:27.475 --> 00:00:31.414
The answer is a curve with a breaking point, and finding it requires loading

00:00:31.414 --> 00:00:33.538
gradually and watching the right metrics.

00:00:34.838 --> 00:00:36.757
k6 works well for this.

00:00:37.057 --> 00:00:42.394
Scripts are code, they run locally and in CI, and thresholds turn the test into a

00:00:42.394 --> 00:00:43.909
pass or fail criterion.

00:00:44.209 --> 00:00:48.585
But a load test against a model has traps of its own: caching that flatters the

00:00:48.585 --> 00:00:53.126
results, provider quota limits that look like saturation and default timeouts that

00:00:53.126 --> 00:00:54.289
cut long answers off.

00:00:54.589 --> 00:00:57.889
This guide builds the test while avoiding those three.

00:00:59.189 --> 00:01:00.887
To follow this guide you need:

00:01:01.437 --> 00:01:03.030
k6 installed.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:07.321
An OpenAI-compatible endpoint and the day 25 targets.

00:01:07.871 --> 00:01:13.365
Approved budget if the API is paid: cap max_tokens, duration and users.

00:01:13.915 --> 00:01:19.633
k6 doesn't natively measure streaming: TTFT under load needs an SSE extension or an

00:01:19.633 --> 00:01:20.872
LLM-specific tool.

00:01:22.172 --> 00:01:23.452
This is the setup.

00:01:23.752 --> 00:01:27.969
Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.

00:01:29.269 --> 00:01:31.280
The same file continues here.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.847
And this is the next setup file.

00:01:36.147 --> 00:01:37.183
Step 1.

00:01:37.483 --> 00:01:39.273
Thresholds from the targets.

00:01:39.823 --> 00:01:43.332
Start from the day 25 baseline and written targets.

00:01:43.632 --> 00:01:45.980
The script's thresholds come from there.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:50.957
The script's thresholds are the targets you wrote yesterday, translated into code:

00:01:50.957 --> 00:01:55.329
the ninety-fifth percentile of time to first token, that of total latency and the

00:01:55.329 --> 00:01:56.355
maximum error rate.

00:01:56.655 --> 00:02:01.348
Don't invent them while writing the script or accept whatever an example brings.

00:02:01.648 --> 00:02:07.200
A threshold with no origin is decoration: nobody knows what it means when it fails.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.272
With product targets as thresholds, a red result means users will have a worse

00:02:12.272 --> 00:02:13.679
experience than agreed.

00:02:14.979 --> 00:02:16.178
Step 2.

00:02:16.478 --> 00:02:18.373
A realistic prompt mix.

00:02:18.923 --> 00:02:21.514
Use a prompt mix similar to the real one.

00:02:21.814 --> 00:02:26.171
With a single repeated prompt, caching gives you numbers production won't see.

00:02:26.721 --> 00:02:31.890
Load prompts from a file with the workload profile's mix: types and proportions.

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:35.327
If you repeat a single prompt, two things happen.

00:02:35.627 --> 00:02:40.426
The provider's cache or your own application's answers far faster than in real use,

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:43.490
and numbers come out better than production will see.

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:49.074
Also, all answers have the same length, and you lose the variability that stresses

00:02:49.074 --> 00:02:49.783
the system.

00:02:50.083 --> 00:02:53.836
Use enough different prompts that they don't repeat during the test.

00:02:55.136 --> 00:02:56.218
Step 3.

00:02:56.518 --> 00:02:58.030
Load in stages.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:01.845
Load in stages: ramp, hold, ramp again.

00:03:02.145 --> 00:03:04.609
Plateaus are where you read behavior.

00:03:05.159 --> 00:03:10.479
Raise load in steps: ramp to a level, hold it for a few minutes, ramp to the next.

00:03:10.779 --> 00:03:15.368
Plateaus are where you read behavior, because the system had time to stabilize.

00:03:15.668 --> 00:03:18.422
During ramps the numbers blend two states.

00:03:18.722 --> 00:03:23.159
Hold each plateau long enough to complete the profile's longest request several

00:03:23.159 --> 00:03:23.496
times.

00:03:23.796 --> 00:03:28.552
If a long answer takes forty seconds, a one-minute plateau barely manages to show

00:03:28.552 --> 00:03:29.139
one round.

00:03:30.439 --> 00:03:31.452
Step 4.

00:03:31.752 --> 00:03:33.032
Finding the knee.

00:03:33.582 --> 00:03:38.295
Look for the knee: the point where total tokens per second stop growing and latency

00:03:38.295 --> 00:03:39.204
starts climbing.

00:03:39.504 --> 00:03:41.201
That's your capacity.

00:03:41.751 --> 00:03:45.469
Plot total tokens per second and latency for each plateau.

00:03:45.769 --> 00:03:50.323
At first, more users produce more tokens per second with stable latency.

00:03:50.623 --> 00:03:54.759
At some point tokens per second stop growing and latency starts climbing.

00:03:55.059 --> 00:03:58.452
That's the knee, and it marks the system's real capacity.

00:03:58.752 --> 00:04:02.945
Beyond that point each additional user only makes the others wait longer.

00:04:03.245 --> 00:04:07.300
Your usable capacity sits a little below it, with headroom for peaks.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:09.729
Step 5.

00:04:10.029 --> 00:04:11.332
Separating errors.

00:04:11.882 --> 00:04:18.188
Separate errors: 429 is the provider's rate limit; 5xx and timeouts are saturation.

00:04:18.488 --> 00:04:20.198
They get fixed differently.

00:04:20.748 --> 00:04:22.759
Not all errors mean the same.

00:04:23.059 --> 00:04:28.016
A 429 is the provider's quota limit: you reached the maximum requests or tokens per

00:04:28.016 --> 00:04:32.794
minute your account allows, and it's solved by requesting more quota or adding a

00:04:32.794 --> 00:04:33.929
queue with retries.

00:04:34.229 --> 00:04:39.775
A 5xx or a timeout is saturation, of your infrastructure or the provider's, and it's

00:04:39.775 --> 00:04:43.275
solved with capacity or by reducing work per request.

00:04:43.575 --> 00:04:45.424
Count them separately in the script.

00:04:45.724 --> 00:04:49.407
Blended into a single error rate, they lead to the wrong fix.

00:04:50.707 --> 00:04:51.894
Step 6.

00:04:52.194 --> 00:04:55.180
Thresholds as the criterion and scheduled runs.

00:04:55.730 --> 00:05:01.374
Keep thresholds as the pass or fail criterion and run the test on a schedule, not on

00:05:01.374 --> 00:05:01.979
every PR.

00:05:02.529 --> 00:05:07.543
Keep thresholds as the automatic pass or fail criterion, so the test can run without

00:05:07.543 --> 00:05:09.811
someone interpreting charts each time.

00:05:10.111 --> 00:05:15.112
Schedule it periodically or before each release, not on every pull request: it's

00:05:15.112 --> 00:05:19.863
slow, costs money if you use a paid API and needs a stable environment to be

00:05:19.863 --> 00:05:20.551
comparable.

00:05:20.851 --> 00:05:22.351
Save each run's summary.

00:05:22.651 --> 00:05:26.739
The trend of the knee over time warns you of a degradation before it becomes an

00:05:26.739 --> 00:05:27.205
incident.

00:05:28.505 --> 00:05:29.611
An example.

00:05:30.161 --> 00:05:34.941
With illustrative figures: a team loads its assistant in plateaus of five, ten,

00:05:34.941 --> 00:05:39.359
twenty and forty users, three minutes each, with fifty different prompts.

00:05:39.659 --> 00:05:44.047
Up to twenty users, tokens per second grow almost proportionally and the

00:05:44.047 --> 00:05:47.521
ninety-fifth percentile of latency holds at nine seconds.

00:05:47.821 --> 00:05:52.419
With forty, tokens per second stay the same as with twenty and latency rises to

00:05:52.419 --> 00:05:53.524
twenty-two seconds.

00:05:53.824 --> 00:05:55.313
The knee is around twenty.

00:05:55.613 --> 00:05:57.787
In the last plateau errors appear.

00:05:58.087 --> 00:06:01.526
Separating them, almost all are 429s.

00:06:01.826 --> 00:06:04.928
It isn't model saturation but the account's quota limit.

00:06:05.228 --> 00:06:09.631
The right action is managing quota and adding a queue, not adding servers.

00:06:10.931 --> 00:06:12.594
The most common mistakes.

00:06:13.144 --> 00:06:17.338
Repeating a single prompt and measuring the cache instead of the system.

00:06:17.888 --> 00:06:20.956
Raising load in a single ramp with no plateaus.

00:06:21.506 --> 00:06:25.850
Adding 429s and 5xxs into a single error rate.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:29.526
Leaving the default timeout and cutting long answers off.

00:06:30.826 --> 00:06:33.069
To know it is done, check this.

00:06:33.619 --> 00:06:36.443
The script's thresholds match the written targets.

00:06:36.993 --> 00:06:40.223
Prompts come from a file with the profile's mix.

00:06:40.774 --> 00:06:43.446
The test has plateaus and you located the knee.

00:06:43.996 --> 00:06:48.690
429 errors are counted apart from 5xxs and timeouts.

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:51.885
And where AI fell short.

00:06:52.435 --> 00:06:58.242
Generated scripts often come with no timeout, no prompt mix and made-up thresholds.

00:06:58.542 --> 00:07:01.320
Review them against your targets before running.

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:04.317
That was day 26.

00:07:04.617 --> 00:07:09.592
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:07:09.592 --> 00:07:10.599
asked questions.

00:07:11.149 --> 00:07:16.690
Continue with day 27: Performance (3): AI generating and analyzing load tests.
